<section id="s6">
  <h2><span class="num">6</span>Discussion and Practical Guidance</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Profile the spectral split before intervening.</strong> One baseline rollout establishes the
    reachable budget; halving it estimates attainable closed-loop recovery. If the reachable share is small,
    no command filter will save your motors and the effort belongs on the reward or the posture instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not eliminate phase lag via delay,</strong> and do not use aggressive cutoffs. Evaluate any
    filter at the top of the speed range, because that is the only place the failure appears.</li>
    <li><strong>Gate by joint load.</strong> The threshold is <i>τ̄</i> ≈ √Var(<i>τ</i>). Gating cost 2.5
    percentage points of heat and bought back most of the tracking.</li>
    <li><strong>Prioritize effort regularization over <code>action_rate</code></strong> [57], [58]. If the goal
    is to protect motors, our evidence says penalise effort, not action difference.</li>
    <li><strong>Expect single-digit percentage gains</strong> on a loaded quadruped, and do not attribute them
    to jitter removal without checking the split.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="callout sig"><p><strong>Where this should pay much better.</strong> The favourable regime — very
  low steady torque — is not represented on a walking quadruped. It is exactly the condition of the reported
  G1 failure: an ankle-roll joint on a standing humanoid. That is where the hardware experiment should look
  first, and it is a prediction this work makes rather than a result it demonstrates.</p></div>
  <p>The 80% power reduction reported for smoothness regularization on a quadrotor [5] does not transfer, and
  we can say why: a rotor has almost no steady holding torque, so its <i>τ̄</i><sup>2</sup> term is small, its
  gait-band term does not exist, and the ripple term dominates by default. A stance-phase knee is the opposite
  case in every one of those three respects.</p>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section id="s7">
  <h2><span class="num">7</span>Related Work</h2>
  <p><strong>Thermal-aware locomotion.</strong> Recent methods add motor temperature to the observation and
  thermal constraints to the reward [2], or move the correction into a residual policy conditioned on thermal
  state [3]. Thermal management by posture was earlier posed as a contact-constrained inverse-kinematics
  problem on a humanoid [9]. Model-free prediction of joint-motor thermal states [52] and lumped-parameter
  thermal networks [41] supply the modelling layer this work proxies rather than models. All retrain or
  instrument; our intervention does neither.</p>

  <p><strong>Actuation interfaces.</strong> Actuator Reality Shaping equips each joint with a
  two-degree-of-freedom controller shaping the physical actuator to match simulation's reference dynamics
  [10]; high-frequency linear feedback at up to 40 kHz interpolates learned torque policies [11]; commanded
  accelerations have been constrained to a voltage-realizable set [12]. Actuator networks learned from bench
  data [22] and domain randomization [34] or online system identification [35] are the standard alternatives.
  Post-optimization of chunked action sequences exists for manipulation [13], [36], [37], as does a B-spline
  action representation [14] and real-time chunking, which handles inference latency by inpainting rather than
  by delaying [38] — the closest prior art to our predictive filter. On cost-constrained quadrupedal hardware
  a measured transport delay above 50 ms is handled with a forward model and a time-aware network [15].
  None of these targets electrical loss.</p>

  <p><strong>Smoothness regularization.</strong> CAPS [5], Lipschitz-constrained policies [16], [48], [54] and
  spectral normalisation [17] all reduce high-frequency content; a benchmark study reports its best hybrid
  method improving control smoothness by 26.8% for a worst-case 2.8% performance degradation [18], and the
  regularizer design space continues to be re-examined [49], [50]. None reports motor current or temperature
  on a legged robot.</p>

  <p><strong>Control rate and discretization.</strong> Robust locomotion has been demonstrated at 8 Hz on
  ANYmal C [19], [32], with low-rate policies shown to be <em>less</em> sensitive to actuation latency;
  Q-learning degenerates as the time step shrinks [20]; delayed MDPs admit augmented-state solutions [46],
  [47]; and policies that choose their own action durations reduce average control frequency without losing
  reward [21]. To our knowledge no paper reports motor temperature or current as a function of policy control
  rate.</p>

  <p><strong>Platforms and stacks.</strong> The quasi-direct-drive actuation that makes copper loss the
  dominant term is established in [30], [31], [56], with series-elastic ANYdrive as the contrasting design
  [32]. Policies of the kind studied here are trained in <code>legged_gym</code> [25] on Isaac Gym [26],
  Isaac Lab [27], MuJoCo Playground [28] or mjlab [29], deployed with stacks such as [33], and derive from the
  teacher-student line of [22], [23], [24].</p>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section id="s8">
  <h2><span class="num">8</span>Limitations</h2>
  <p><strong>No hardware.</strong> Everything here is simulation. 𝔼[τ²] is proportional to copper loss only
  under a constant torque constant; it ignores iron loss, inverter loss and every thermal path. The claims are
  about a <em>proxy</em>.</p>
  <p><strong>The single-joint model has no contact.</strong> E1 and E2 use a second-order joint with a constant
  load. Real stance-phase torque is neither constant nor independent of the command.</p>
  <p><strong>One robot, one gait family, one policy family,</strong> all from the same training setup on flat
  terrain.</p>
  <p><strong>The 5 Hz band split is a modelling choice, and the headline number depends on it.</strong> A lower
  boundary would make the reachable share larger and a higher one smaller. The 25 Hz boundary, by contrast, is
  not a choice: it is the command Nyquist. A sweep of the 5 Hz line would make the budget claim considerably
  more solid, and we have not done it.</p>
  <p><strong>"Reachable" is an upper bound, not an achievable target.</strong> Some 5–25 Hz torque content is
  the robot's genuine response to contact, not command jitter, and removing it costs stability. The recovery
  ratio of about a half may be exactly this: the fraction of the band that is truly discretionary.</p>
  <p><strong>The reward-ablation evidence is single-seed,</strong> and success is measured as tracking, not as
  task completion over long horizons.</p>
  <p><strong>Depth of reading of the cited literature.</strong> Existence, venue and authorship of every
  reference in §10 were verified by search during preparation. That is a weaker claim than having read each in
  full, and §7's characterisations should be read accordingly.</p>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section id="s9">
  <h2><span class="num">9</span>Conclusion</h2>
  <p class="lead">Actuator overheating in learned legged locomotion decomposes into steady posture torque and
  dynamic command ripple. Because these components combine in quadrature, command filtering provides
  meaningful thermal relief only on lightly loaded joints operating below the load crossover threshold. In
  closed loop, phase lag introduced by smoothing compromises feedback stability at speed, demonstrating that
  causal predictive filtering with load-dependent gating is required to safely recover actuator thermal
  margins.</p>
</section>

